With a first baby, people show up. There are showers, meal trains, visits, lots of questions and even more advice.

With a second or third baby, something shifts. The gifts get smaller, the visits get shorter, and there is a quiet assumption that you already know what you are doing. You have done this before, after all.

But you have never done it with this baby, in this body, with this family. You are still a new mother.

## The world assumes you already know

I am a mother of three, and every one of my postpartum seasons was different. My third was the hardest. I had more experience than ever, and I still found myself searching for support and answers in the middle of the night.

Experience helps. It does not make you immune. Every birth is different, every recovery is different, and every baby arrives with their own temperament and their own needs. What worked last time might not work now, and that does not mean you forgot how to be a mother.

## What is different this time

This time, you are recovering while caring for more than one child. You might be feeding a newborn through the night and getting an older child out the door in the morning. Your older child may need extra reassurance just when you have the least left to give.

There is less room to rest, too. The first time, maybe you could nap when the baby napped. Now there is a toddler, or homework, or a whole household that keeps running whether you slept or not.

And there is often less support. Many families lean on help the first time and assume they will not need it again. Sometimes the people who helped before are simply not available now. Some mothers are also far from their own families, carrying a new baby without the mother, aunt or neighbor who would have been there back home.

Your body counts, too. Recovery after a second or third birth deserves the same attention as the first. If something feels different or not right, bring it to your OB or midwife, even if a voice inside says you are supposed to know by now.

## You deserve care this time, too

I believe every new mother deserves to be cared for and supported, no matter how many times she has gone through it.

Care in a second or third postpartum can be very practical. Someone who helps include the older siblings, so they do not feel pushed aside. Someone who prepares a meal, folds the laundry, or holds the baby so you can give your older child your full attention for an hour. Someone who asks how you are, and waits for the real answer.

It can also be emotional. Many mothers feel guilt toward the older child, grief for the way things used to be, or worry about whether there is enough love to go around. These feelings are common. They are allowed. And they tend to feel lighter once they are said out loud.

You are not a beginner, and nobody expects you to be an expert either. You are a mother meeting a new person, again, in a body that is healing, again.

That is still new. And whether this is your first baby or your third, you deserve to be cared for, too.
